.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_RNG 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_xoshiro_init ,
.Nm xft_xoshiro256ss
.Nd xoshiro256** pseudo-random number generator
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In rng.h
.Ft void
.Fn xft_xoshiro_init "t_xoshiro xoshiro"
.Ft t_u64a
.Fn xft_xoshiro256ss "t_xoshiro xoshiro"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In rng.h
provides a 256-bit state pseudorandom generator.
It does not depend on a global seed and has no locking; a
.Vt t_xoshiro
value is a plain four-word array the caller owns.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef t_u64a t_xoshiro[4];
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn xft_xoshiro_init
seeds the four words of
.Fa xoshiro .
It mixes a private static counter, the address of
.Fa xoshiro ,
and the current monotonic time from
.Xr xft_timing 3
into a starting value, then runs that value through four invocations of the
SplitMix64 finalizer.
Each resulting word is OR-ed with a small odd constant
.Po
1, 2, 4, 8
.Pc
so that the four lanes start out distinct and none of them is zero.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xoshiro256ss
advances the state and returns 64 bits.
The generator is the standard xoshiro256** except for the multiplier used
in the output function; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t3  = s[1] + (s[1] << 1);       /* 3 * s[1] */
res = rotl64(t3, 7);
res = res + (res << 3);         /* 9 * rotl64(t3, 7) */
t   = s[1] << 17;
s[2] ^= s[0];
s[3] ^= s[1];
s[1] ^= s[2];
s[0] ^= s[3];
s[2] ^= t;
s[3]  = rotl64(s[3], 45);
return (res);
.Ed
.Pp
The state update is performed after the output value is computed, so the
first call after
.Fn xft_xoshiro_init
uses the seeded state directly.
.Pp
Neither function allocates.
.Fn xft_xoshiro256ss
mutates
.Fa xoshiro
in place; the caller is responsible for keeping each generator's state on a
single thread.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_xoshiro256ss
returns 64 pseudo-random bits.
.Fn xft_xoshiro_init
returns nothing.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_u64	roll_die(t_xoshiro state)
{
	return (xft_xoshiro256ss(state) % 6 + 1);
}

t_u64	random_u64(void)
{
	static t_xoshiro	state;
	static bool			seeded = false;

	if (!seeded)
	{
		xft_xoshiro_init(state);
		seeded = true;
	}
	return (xft_xoshiro256ss(state));
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_fuzzer 3 ,
.Xr xft_hash 3 ,
.Xr xft_timing 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
The state transition deviates from the published xoshiro256** algorithm in
the output function: the reference implementation rotates
.Ql s[1] * 5 ,
while this library rotates
.Ql s[1] * 3 .
A given state therefore does not produce the same sequence as a reference
implementation seeded identically.
.Pp
The output of
.Fn xft_xoshiro256ss
is not cryptographically secure.
It is suitable for simulation, hashing seeds and fuzz input, not for keys
or nonces.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xoshiro_init
is not thread safe because of the static counter it updates.
Calling it from two threads concurrently can produce two generators with
the same initial state.
.Pp
.Fn xft_xoshiro_init
calls
.Fn xft_get_nanos ,
which on x86_64 lazily calibrates the cycle counter.
The first call is significantly slower than the rest.
